shadowfetch-fireproof (2.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release, for Shadowfetch Linux 2.1.2 "Fire Edition".
  * Analyze is side-effect free: opening the update screen takes no apt
    lock and downloads nothing. The upgrade is simulated on a read-only
    python3-apt cache (same libapt-pkg as apt, so the answer is
    identical), with a kept-back story from the safe-upgrade diff, holds
    shown as "pinned, will not update", and libfoo1 -> libfoo1t64 pairs
    collapsed to one "same library, new name" line.
  * The dpkg frontend lock is taken only at commit, and the simulation is
    re-run under it: if the archive moved since the user read the list,
    the lock is released and analyze re-renders instead of installing
    something nobody approved. Idling on the analyze screen holds nothing
    against Discover or terminal apt.
  * Zero Fireproof snapshots: snapper's shipped 80snapper apt hooks (and
    Phoenix's 79phoenix-space) wrap the commit. Fireproof records the
    highest snapper number beforehand, names the FIRST type=pre snapshot
    of the transaction as the rollback target, and relabels it with
    fireproof=pre,txn=<uuid> userdata so the Phoenix panel and the boot
    menu agree on the Point.
  * Post-update verify battery, headless and in seconds: dpkg --audit,
    apt -f consistency, initrd freshness per new kernel, grub.cfg, dkms
    module state (the black-screen preventer with nvidia-kernel-dkms),
    systemctl --failed diffed against a pre-commit baseline, needrestart,
    default route + mirror resolution, and a WARN-only graphics scan - a
    graphical login is never claimed before a reboot.
  * First-boot-after-update protocol: a pending flag written before the
    reboot offer, cleared by fireproof-postboot.service when a real
    graphical session appears (120 s fallback timer for boots that never
    get there). A failed boot opens recovery preselecting the recorded
    pre-Point, and an /etc/issue.d drop-in prints the exact
    phoenix-restore command for the no-GUI case.
  * Rollback carousel closed: the package-set hash of any rolled-back or
    verify-failed transaction is recorded; the same pending set
    re-analyzes with the default flipped to Don't proceed and its login
    notification suppressed. A different set clears the match naturally.
  * Update ownership: DiscoverNotifier hidden for new users by the
    live-build skel override (owned by the ISO, not this package),
    packagekit-offline-update.service masked as a backstop,
    APT::Periodic refreshes package lists only - nothing downloads or
    installs unattended, ever. The session autostart check posts the
    proactive login notification when updates are pending and the
    rollback carousel has not suppressed it.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 12:00:00 -0400
